<td>Tracked per client, per service, month over month</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h2 id="cancellation-policy">A Cancellation Policy Clients Actually See</h2>
<p>A policy that only exists in a staff member&#8217;s memory isn&#8217;t a policy — it&#8217;s a source of awkward disputes. The fix is simple: put it somewhere a client will actually see it before they book, not just where you can point to it afterward.</p>
<ul>
<li>State it on the booking page itself, next to the &#8220;confirm&#8221; button — not buried in a separate terms page</li>
<li>Repeat it, briefly, in the WhatsApp confirmation message</li>
<li>Keep the tone matter-of-fact rather than defensive: &#8220;A small deposit secures your slot and is credited toward your service&#8221; reads very differently from a wall of legal-sounding text</li>
</ul>
<p>Clients rarely object to a policy stated plainly and applied consistently. What creates friction is inconsistency — waiving it for some clients and enforcing it for others, or only mentioning it after someone has already missed an appointment.</p>
<h2 id="repeat-offenders">Handling Repeat No-Show Clients</h2>
<p>Most no-shows are one-offs from otherwise good clients — genuine emergencies, forgotten appointments, plans that changed. A small minority are repeat offenders, and they deserve a different response than a first-time miss.</p>
<p>A practical approach: track no-shows per client (most modern booking software does this automatically), and after a second no-show within a short window, move that client to prepayment-only for future bookings — communicated politely, not as a punishment, but as &#8220;for future bookings, we ask for prepayment to secure your slot.&#8221; This protects the chairs and the stylists&#8217; time without turning into a confrontation, and it quietly filters out the small number of clients who were costing the salon money every month.</p>
<p>Once this system is running well, it also feeds directly into your reputation — clients who show up reliably tend to be the same ones leaving reviews, which ties back into the review engine covered in <a href="/salon-marketing-dubai-guide/">the complete salon marketing guide for Dubai</a>.</p>
<div class="gd-faq" id="salon-no-shows-faq">
<h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>
<h4>Will charging a deposit scare away new clients?</h4>
<p>In practice, a small, clearly-stated deposit rarely deters genuine bookings — it mainly filters out the casual, low-intent bookings that were most likely to no-show anyway. The key is communicating it plainly at the point of booking, not springing it on the client afterward.</p>
<h4>What&#8217;s a reasonable no-show rate for a Dubai salon?</h4>
<p>Most well-run salons with a deposit and reminder system in place see no-show rates in the low single digits. Salons with no system at all often run considerably higher without realising it, because the loss is never tracked as a specific number.</p>
<h4>Should the reminder come by SMS, WhatsApp, or email?</h4>
<p>WhatsApp consistently gets read and replied to fastest in the UAE market. Email is easy to ignore for an appointment reminder, and SMS works but lacks the two-way &#8220;reply to confirm or reschedule&#8221; convenience WhatsApp offers.</p>
<h4>How do I introduce a deposit policy without upsetting existing regular clients?</h4>
<p>Announce it clearly in advance — a WhatsApp broadcast or an Instagram post explaining the change and why — rather than applying it silently. Most regulars who show up reliably won&#8217;t be affected in practice and appreciate the transparency.</p>
<h4>Is a waitlist worth setting up if my salon is already usually fully booked?</h4>
<p>Yes — a fully booked salon is exactly where a waitlist pays off most, because every cancellation that goes unfilled is a guaranteed loss rather than a maybe. The busier the salon, the faster a waitlist slot gets claimed.</p>
</div>
